# Vue patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design-pattern guidance to
idiomatic Vue 3 Composition API design and its common anti-patterns.
Applies to `*.vue` files and plain `*.ts` files alike (R1 review, PR #719,
M4): a composable (`useX()`) is routinely a standalone `.ts` file, not a
`.vue` SFC, and the reactivity/composable guidance below (`ref()` vs.
`reactive()`, lifecycle-hook-in-composables rules, the return-shape rule)
is exactly what a `.ts` composable needs — `ts-js-node`'s own generic
patterns rule has no notion of Vue's reactivity primitives at all.

## Reactivity

- Use `ref()` for a single primitive or a value that is often reassigned
  wholesale; use `reactive()` for a fixed-shape object whose properties are
  mutated in place. Do not wrap a `reactive()` object in a `ref()` "for
  safety" -- pick one.
- Never destructure a `reactive()` object or a raw (non-`defineProps`)
  props object into loose variables (`const { count } = state`) -- that
  copies the current value and breaks the reactive link. Use `toRefs()`
  (whole object) or `toRef()` (one property) when a template or composable
  needs individual reactive references out of a `reactive()` object.
- Prefer a `computed()` over a `watch` that just re-derives and assigns a
  value on every dependency change; reach for `watch`/`watchEffect` only
  for a genuine side effect (a fetch, a DOM measurement, a log, syncing to
  an external system).
- Give every `watch` an explicit, minimal source (`watch(() => props.id,
  ...)`, not `watch(props, ...)` with `{ deep: true }` when only one field
  actually needs to be observed) -- a whole-object deep watch fires on every
  unrelated field change and is expensive on large objects.
- Stop a `watch`/`watchEffect` created outside a component's own setup
  scope (e.g. inside a manually managed subscription) using the stop handle
  it returns; one created directly inside `setup`/`<script setup>` is
  auto-stopped on unmount and needs no manual cleanup.

## Composables

- Extract a composable (`useX()`) when the same stateful logic (a fetch +
  loading/error state, a subscription, a computed derivation) is needed in
  more than one component, instead of copy-pasting the `ref`/`watch` block.
- Call composables that use lifecycle hooks (`onMounted`, `onUnmounted`,
  `provide`/`inject`) only from another composable or directly inside
  `setup`/`<script setup>` -- never from inside a callback, `watch`
  handler, or `async` continuation after an `await`, where the active
  component instance is no longer bound.
- A composable returns refs/computed values and functions, not a plain
  object of dereferenced values -- return `{ count, increment }` (where
  `count` is the `ref` itself), not `{ count: count.value, increment }`,
  or the caller loses reactivity.

## Component design

- Emit an event (`emit('update:modelValue', v)`) instead of mutating a
  prop in place; a prop is one-way data flow from the parent, and Vue warns
  at runtime when a child writes to it directly.
- Use `v-model` (or a named `v-model:propName`) for two-way bindable state
  instead of hand-wiring a prop plus a separately-named update event when
  the semantics are genuinely two-way.
- Keep template `v-if`/`v-for` on the same element simple; when both are
  needed, wrap the `v-for` in a `<template>` with the `v-if` on each item,
  or filter the source list in a `computed()`, rather than relying on
  `eslint-plugin-vue`'s default precedence to make an implicit filter
  correct.
- Prefer `provide`/`inject` for state genuinely shared down an arbitrary
  depth of the component tree (theme, current user); prop-drilling two or
  three levels does not need it, and a store (Pinia) is the better fit for
  state shared across unrelated branches of the tree.

## Anti-patterns

- A `ref`/`reactive` object stored in module scope and mutated from
  multiple unrelated components as an ad hoc global store, instead of a
  Pinia store with named actions -- it works but loses devtools tracking,
  action naming, and testability.
- A deeply nested `reactive()` object mutated from many call sites with no
  action layer -- prefer a Pinia store (or a composable with named mutator
  functions) so state changes have one discoverable entry point.
